2. Why does a Blade form that updates a record need @method('PUT') and @csrf? What happens without each one?
   when we define a route, we also define which http method is allowed against that route. For eg get, post, put, patch or delete. A normal HTML Form allows us to send through GET and POST methods. But laravel provides us the directive @method()
   Through which we can send PUT, PATCH, DELETE methods in the form according to what the route requires.
   @csrf is a blade directive provided by laravel which is used to protect the form from the csrf realted attacks.
   The directive automatically generates the csrf token when it is used in the form.
   If a request does not does not contain a correct csrf token it gives the 419 code error (Page Expired) to the user. Does protecting us against the malicious requests.

